\section*{Bounded Complements for \$\textbackslash{}varepsilon-Log-Canonical\$ Varieties}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Fano contractions with a fixed positive log-discrepancy gap are claimed to admit locally bounded-index klt complements, with the stated monotone finite-coefficient extension over the complex numbers. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Fix $d\ge1$ and rational $\varepsilon>0$. OpenAI claims an integer $N(d,\varepsilon)>0$ for all projective surjective connected-fiber contractions $f:X\to Z$ of normal integral varieties over a field that has characteristic zero and is algebraically closed, where $\dim X=d$, $K_X$ is rational Cartier, $X$ is $\varepsilon$-log canonical and $-K_X$ is relatively ample. For every closed $z\in Z$, it claims effective rational $B$ and a Zariski neighborhood $U$ of $z$ such that $(X,B)$ is klt over $U$ and
\[
N(K_X+B)|_{f^{-1}(U)}\text{ is integral Cartier and linearly equivalent to }0.
\]
The bound is uniform in the contraction and point, and the complement is klt rather than merely log canonical.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 066, 2026-09-25): The problem is reduced to a birational threshold contradiction while a bounded lc complement supplies an auxiliary graded section system. Constrained cone valuations retain exact divisor normalization, and two-stage Cartier-trace lifting provides controlled sections whose rank growth would otherwise force a prime with discrepancy below the prescribed gap.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 066, 2026-09-25): The manuscript assumes a positive epsilon-lc Fano contraction and gives a bounded klt complement locally over the base. The problem includes epsilon zero, a nef-and-big rather than ample setting, and an epsilon-lc complement. Those scope and retained-singularity conditions are not all covered.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 066 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

\subsection{Sources}
